You can skip the VM. Container Apps can host the app without one. You'd just lose the "hardened a Linux server with Ansible to a CIS/STIG baseline" part of your story. A free middle ground is a local VM on your PC through Hyper-V or Multipass, which gives you a practice target for Ansible and hardening without any cloud cost. I'd leave this decision for later. It isn't needed until well after the app works.

Plan for your next sessions

Stay in order, because each step produces the next step's inputs. And design comes before code; that's the SSDLC point you're making.

Finish acceptance criteria and add security criteria.
Write each criterion in Given/When/Then format so it can later become a test.
For each story, add at least one security criterion. For example: "Given a client, when they call the ban endpoint, then the server returns 403." Also cover input validation and rate limits.
Write a few misuse cases, like "a client books a class for someone else" or "a user replays a Stripe webhook."
Deliverable: a complete set of stories with functional and security acceptance criteria.
Scope the MVP and set up the backlog.
Sort the stories into must, should, could and won't (the MoSCoW method), and choose the smallest version Emma could actually use.
Classify your data: what's personal information, and what payment data Stripe handles so you never touch card numbers.
Load the must-have stories into GitHub Issues with a GitHub Projects board. That's your Agile/Jira experience.
Deliverable: the MVP backlog and a data classification table.
Design.
Draw an architecture diagram of the Azure layout we discussed.
Design the Postgres schema as an ERD: users, roles, classes, sessions, bookings, payments.
Sketch the API endpoints.
Write short decision records (ADRs) for the big choices, like Postgres over MongoDB and Container Apps over a full Kubernetes cluster. These records are great interview material.
Deliverable: the diagram, ERD, API outline and ADRs.
Threat model.
Draw a data flow diagram and walk through it with STRIDE.
Each threat you find becomes a new acceptance criterion or a test, which ties back to step 1.
Update your traceability matrix, which links each requirement to its design, test and mitigation. This lines up directly with your CSSLP.
Deliverable: the threat model and updated requirements.
Dev environment.
Set up WSL2, the GitHub repo, branch protection, and a pre-commit hook that runs Gitleaks to catch secrets.
Get a basic "hello world" API talking to Postgres in Docker Compose.
Deliverable: docker compose up runs the skeleton app.
CI pipeline and first feature.
Set up GitHub Actions to run lint, tests, Semgrep and Trivy on every pull request.
Build the admin ban/unban flow with server-side authorization. Its acceptance criteria from step 1 become the tests.
Deliverable: the first feature merged through a pipeline that enforces security checks.

After step 6, the app is real enough to add to your resume. Terraform, Azure deployment and Kubernetes come after that.


